$ABCD$ is a cyclic quadrilateral with $\angle DAB=104^{\circ}$ and $\angle ABC=82^{\circ}$. Name the angle opposite $\angle ABC$, then find the other two interior angles. Check that the four angles sum to $360^{\circ}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (150:2);
\coordinate (B) at (70:2);
\coordinate (C) at (-46:2);
\coordinate (D) at (-138:2);
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below left] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$ABCD$ is a cyclic quadrilateral. Side $BC$ is extended through $C$ to $E$, and $\angle DCE=100^{\circ}$. Find $\angle DAB$ and the adjacent interior angle $\angle BCD$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (150:2);
\coordinate (B) at (70:2);
\coordinate (C) at (-10:2);
\coordinate (D) at (-130:2);
\coordinate (E) at ($(B)!1.45!(C)$);
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (C)--(E);
\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above] at (B) {$B$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below left] at (D) {$D$};
\node[below right] at (E) {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$ABCD$ is a cyclic quadrilateral. Side $DA$ is extended through $A$ to $E$, and side $CD$ is extended through $D$ to $F$. Given $\angle BAE=78^{\circ}$ and $\angle ADF=84^{\circ}$, find all four interior angles.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (150:2);
\coordinate (B) at (70:2);
\coordinate (C) at (-42:2);
\coordinate (D) at (-134:2);
\coordinate (E) at ($(D)!1.35!(A)$);
\coordinate (F) at ($(C)!1.35!(D)$);
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A)--(E);
\draw (D)--(F);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below] at (D) {$D$};
\node[above left] at (E) {$E$};
\node[below left] at (F) {$F$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$KLMN$ is a cyclic quadrilateral. Side $NK$ is extended through $K$ to $T$. Prove that $\angle LKT=\angle LMN$ using supplementary angles.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (K) at (150:2);
\coordinate (L) at (70:2);
\coordinate (M) at (-10:2);
\coordinate (N) at (-110:2);
\coordinate (T) at ($(N)!1.35!(K)$);
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\draw (K)--(L)--(M)--(N)--cycle;
\draw (K)--(T);
\node[left] at (K) {$K$};
\node[above right] at (L) {$L$};
\node[right] at (M) {$M$};
\node[below] at (N) {$N$};
\node[above left] at (T) {$T$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
